.TH CCOL_CIRCULAR_QUEUE_CREATE 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
ccol_circular_queue_create \- create a circular queue
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cthreadcomm.h>

ccol_circular_queue_create(max_size, err_str);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
This macro creates a queue with a fixed size, built on a circular
buffer. A send blocks while the queue is full and waits for free space.
The macro uses the standard malloc and free for memory management.

This macro is the short form of ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs()
with NULL memory management procedures.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
max_size  Maximum number of messages that the queue can hold. The value
          must be from 1 to SIZE_MAX / sizeof(c_message_t), and the
          macro refuses a larger value: one array of
          max_size * sizeof(c_message_t) bytes holds the queue, and a
          larger value makes that computation overflow.
err_str   Optional pointer that gets an error string on failure
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
On success, the macro gives a pointer to the new circular queue.
On failure, it gives NULL.
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
All operations on this queue are thread-safe.
You must destroy the queue with ccol_circular_queue_destroy() after you
finish with it.
There are two supported ways to combine fork(2) with this queue: call
fork(2) before you create the queue, or create the queue, then call
fork(2) and immediately call exec(3). One pattern is not supported: a
program that continues to run with no exec(3) in between while a queue
from before the fork is live. On top of that rule, the library adds a
defense in depth: the child never inherits a lock in the locked state
from another thread that held it at the instant of the fork.  Because
the queue also owns no thread of its own, a queue that a child inherits
in that way stays fully usable.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR ccol_circular_queue_create_with_mprocs (3),
.BR ccol_circular_queue_destroy (3)
